---
title: webserv — One request, one response (lifecycle)
---

%%{init: {"theme": "dark"}}%%

%% =====================================================================
%% The story of a single browser hitting webserv:
%%   TCP connect → accept → recv bytes → parse → route →
%%   build response → send bytes → keep-alive or close.
%%
%% Read top to bottom. Numbers in the left margin are the order things
%% happen. The poll() calls between steps are not separate "actions" —
%% they're just where the loop sleeps waiting for the OS to say
%% "this fd is ready now". Plain English first, then the C++ symbol you
%% can grep for in src/server/Server.cpp.
%% =====================================================================

sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Browser as Browser
    participant Listen as Listen socket (port 9090)
    participant Server as Server::run() — poll() loop
    participant Conn as Connection (one per client fd)
    participant Req as HttpRequest (parser)
    participant Resp as HttpResponse (builder)
    participant Disk as Disk (./www/)

    Note over Server,Listen: At startup, addListener(9090) made this socket<br/>non-blocking and registered it with poll() watching POLLIN.

    Browser->>Listen: TCP SYN (connect)
    Note over Server: poll() wakes: POLLIN on the listen fd.
    Server->>Server: acceptNewClient(fd)<br/>accept() → new client fd, set O_NONBLOCK
    Server->>Conn: new Connection(fd)<br/>state = READING_REQUEST
    Note over Server: add client fd to poll set, watching POLLIN.

    Browser->>Server: send bytes — the request line, headers, blank line<br/>("GET / HTTP/1.1" + Host header + CRLF CRLF)
    Note over Server: poll() wakes: POLLIN on the client fd.
    Server->>Server: handleReadable(fd)<br/>recv() up to 4096 bytes (READ_CHUNK)
    Server->>Conn: touch() — reset the 30 s idle timer
    Server->>Req: feed(buf, n)<br/>parser eats bytes — partial reads are fine

    alt parser hit an error (bad syntax, too big, etc.)
        Req-->>Server: state = ERROR_STATE, errorCode = 400 / 413 / 414 / 431 / 501 / 505
        Server->>Resp: buildErrorResponse(code)<br/>(Connection: close, no keep-alive)
    else parser still needs more bytes
        Note over Server: nothing to do — just return.<br/>poll() will wake again when more bytes arrive.
    else parser finished a whole request
        Req-->>Server: state = COMPLETE<br/>method · uri · headers · body filled in
        Server->>Server: handleRequest(c)<br/>see webserv-routing.mermaid
        Server->>Disk: stat / open / read (or write / remove for upload / delete)
        Note over Server,Disk: Disk I/O is exempt from poll().<br/>You may read/write regular files directly.
        Disk-->>Server: file bytes (or "not found")
        Server->>Resp: setStatus / setHeader / setBody
    end

    Resp-->>Conn: writeBuf = resp.serialize()<br/>bytesSent = 0
    Server->>Conn: state = WRITING_RESPONSE
    Note over Server: setPollEvents(fd, POLLOUT) —<br/>now we want the kernel to tell us when we can send.

    Note over Server: poll() wakes: POLLOUT on the client fd.
    Server->>Browser: send() bytes from writeBuf+bytesSent
    Server->>Conn: addBytesSent(n)

    loop until writeBuf is fully drained
        Note over Server: poll() wakes again on POLLOUT.<br/>The OS only takes as many bytes as its<br/>send buffer has room for — that's why we loop.
        Server->>Browser: send() more bytes
        Server->>Conn: addBytesSent(n)
    end

    alt shouldKeepAlive() is true (HTTP/1.1 default, no "Connection: close")
        Server->>Conn: resetForNextRequest()<br/>clears HttpRequest, buffers,<br/>state = READING_REQUEST
        Note over Server: setPollEvents(fd, POLLIN) —<br/>same TCP socket, ready for the next request.
    else explicit close, or it was an error response
        Server->>Server: closeConnection(fd)<br/>close(fd); erase from _conns;<br/>remove from poll set
    end

    Browser-->>Browser: render response / next request

    Note over Server: every loop iteration also runs checkTimeouts():<br/>any Connection with lastActivity > 30 s ago → closeConnection.<br/>This is how webserv guarantees no client can hang forever.
